1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,148
During the Second World War,

2
00:00:02,168 --> 00:00:04,066
not far from the Pentagon,

3
00:00:04,086 --> 00:00:07,134
German prisoners sat in small rooms and talked.

4
00:00:07,154 --> 00:00:08,668
U-boat officers, army officers,

5
00:00:08,688 --> 00:00:09,052
scientists.

6
00:00:09,072 --> 00:00:10,970
They talked about the war,

7
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:14,038
and about the Americans who had captured them.

8
00:00:14,058 --> 00:00:16,723
They thought they were only talking to

9
00:00:16,743 --> 00:00:17,543
each other.

10
00:00:18,725 --> 00:00:20,501
In another building, young American

11
00:00:20,521 --> 00:00:23,374
soldiers in headphones were writing it all down.

12
00:00:23,394 --> 00:00:26,965
Many of them had been born in Germany or Austria.

13
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:29,479
Many were Jewish, and had escaped the

14
00:00:29,499 --> 00:00:31,634
Nazis only a few years before.

15
00:00:31,654 --> 00:00:34,507
Now they were listening to the men they

16
00:00:34,527 --> 00:00:35,327
had fled.

17
00:00:36,575 --> 00:00:39,094
The place did not officially exist.

18
00:00:39,114 --> 00:00:42,479
It had no name, only a mailing address:

19
00:00:42,499 --> 00:00:42,902
P.O.

20
00:00:42,922 --> 00:00:43,749
Box 1142.

21
00:00:43,769 --> 00:00:46,711
This is the documented story of America's

22
00:00:46,731 --> 00:00:50,096
secret listening post, and of the men who

23
00:00:50,116 --> 00:00:52,655
kept its secret for sixty years.

24
00:00:54,375 --> 00:00:56,475
The address belonged to Fort Hunt,

25
00:00:56,495 --> 00:00:58,948
an old coastal artillery fort on the

26
00:00:58,968 --> 00:01:00,362
Potomac River in Virginia,

27
00:01:00,382 --> 00:01:03,542
on land that had once been part of George

28
00:01:03,562 --> 00:01:05,308
Washington's estate at Mount Vernon.

29
00:01:05,328 --> 00:01:09,220
In April 1942, the Army began using it to

30
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:10,040
question prisoners.

31
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:13,368
The site was chosen with care.

32
00:01:13,388 --> 00:01:16,351
It was isolated enough to keep a secret,

33
00:01:16,371 --> 00:01:19,334
and close enough to the Pentagon to send

34
00:01:19,354 --> 00:01:21,199
what it learned there quickly.

35
00:01:21,219 --> 00:01:23,436
At its height, there were eighty-seven

36
00:01:23,456 --> 00:01:24,928
buildings behind the wire.

37
00:01:24,948 --> 00:01:27,165
Neighbors saw unmarked buses with darkened

38
00:01:27,185 --> 00:01:29,050
windows driving in and out.

39
00:01:30,275 --> 00:01:34,006
On paper, it was not a prison camp at all.

40
00:01:34,026 --> 00:01:35,882
It was a processing center.

41
00:01:35,902 --> 00:01:36,632
That mattered.

42
00:01:36,652 --> 00:01:39,633
Prisoners could be held for days or weeks

43
00:01:39,653 --> 00:01:42,259
before anyone told the Red Cross where

44
00:01:42,279 --> 00:01:43,009
they were.

45
00:01:43,029 --> 00:01:44,509
Under the Geneva Convention,

46
00:01:44,529 --> 00:01:47,155
the Red Cross was supposed to know.

47
00:01:48,825 --> 00:01:51,505
To make it work, the Army needed something

48
00:01:51,525 --> 00:01:51,843
rare:

49
00:01:51,863 --> 00:01:54,543
soldiers who spoke German the way Germans did.

50
00:01:54,563 --> 00:01:57,244
Many of the people it found were refugees.

51
00:01:57,264 --> 00:01:59,944
They had fled Nazi Germany and Austria as

52
00:01:59,964 --> 00:02:01,969
boys, often because they were Jewish,

53
00:02:01,989 --> 00:02:05,365
and they had every reason to want the war won.

54
00:02:06,625 --> 00:02:08,972
The Navy was just as choosy.

55
00:02:08,992 --> 00:02:11,338
It interviewed four hundred and fifty

56
00:02:11,358 --> 00:02:12,916
candidates for interrogation work,

57
00:02:12,936 --> 00:02:14,099
and took thirty-five.

58
00:02:14,119 --> 00:02:17,649
Some of the Army's men had trained at Camp

59
00:02:17,669 --> 00:02:20,016
Ritchie, in Maryland, the school for

60
00:02:20,036 --> 00:02:20,836
military intelligence.

61
00:02:22,150 --> 00:02:24,473
One of them was Peter Weiss,

62
00:02:24,493 --> 00:02:26,815
a young sergeant born in Vienna.

63
00:02:26,835 --> 00:02:29,939
Decades later he described his job in a

64
00:02:29,959 --> 00:02:30,720
single sentence.

65
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:33,453
My job, he said, was to listen,

66
00:02:33,473 --> 00:02:35,014
and listen very carefully.

67
00:02:35,034 --> 00:02:37,357
Sometimes that listening happened when the

68
00:02:37,377 --> 00:02:40,110
prisoners did not know anyone was listening.

69
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:45,630
Between 1942 and the summer of 1945,

70
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:48,294
more than three thousand four hundred

71
00:02:48,314 --> 00:02:50,070
prisoners passed through P.O.

72
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:50,958
Box 1142.

73
00:02:50,978 --> 00:02:53,178
They were not ordinary soldiers.

74
00:02:53,198 --> 00:02:56,730
They were picked for what they might know:

75
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:58,950
U-boat commanders and their crews,

76
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:01,190
senior officers, engineers and scientists.

77
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:04,909
Among them was General Reinhard Gehlen,

78
00:03:04,929 --> 00:03:07,288
who had run German intelligence against

79
00:03:07,308 --> 00:03:10,064
the Soviet army on the Eastern Front.

80
00:03:10,084 --> 00:03:12,840
For the Americans, men like him were

81
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,219
becoming more valuable by the month.

82
00:03:15,239 --> 00:03:17,994
The war against Germany was being won.

83
00:03:18,014 --> 00:03:20,790
The next rival was already in view.

84
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,064
The rooms where the prisoners lived and

85
00:03:25,084 --> 00:03:27,278
talked were wired with hidden microphones.

86
00:03:27,298 --> 00:03:29,862
At first, even some American officers were

87
00:03:29,882 --> 00:03:30,969
uneasy about it.

88
00:03:30,989 --> 00:03:33,922
There was an old feeling that gentlemen do

89
00:03:33,942 --> 00:03:35,767
not read each other's mail.

90
00:03:35,787 --> 00:03:38,740
But the results were hard to argue with.

91
00:03:39,975 --> 00:03:41,576
The interrogations were useful.

92
00:03:41,596 --> 00:03:43,602
The recordings were often better.

93
00:03:43,622 --> 00:03:46,033
Some of the most important discoveries

94
00:03:46,053 --> 00:03:48,465
came not from questioning at all,

95
00:03:48,485 --> 00:03:50,491
but from prisoners talking among

96
00:03:50,511 --> 00:03:52,922
themselves, sure that they were alone.

97
00:03:52,942 --> 00:03:54,948
There were also stool pigeons:

98
00:03:54,968 --> 00:03:57,785
men placed among the prisoners to steer

99
00:03:57,805 --> 00:03:58,615
the conversation.

100
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:01,968
Not every prisoner was fooled.

101
00:04:01,988 --> 00:04:04,295
Some guessed the rooms were bugged,

102
00:04:04,315 --> 00:04:06,233
and talked endlessly about nothing,

103
00:04:06,253 --> 00:04:08,947
leaving American clerks to type up hours

104
00:04:08,967 --> 00:04:10,130
of meaningless chatter.

105
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,949
The veterans of Fort Hunt insisted that

106
00:04:14,969 --> 00:04:17,141
they did not beat anyone.

107
00:04:17,161 --> 00:04:19,332
Instead, they rewarded prisoners who

108
00:04:19,352 --> 00:04:21,086
cooperated, with books, newspapers,

109
00:04:21,106 --> 00:04:22,401
cigarettes and liquor.

110
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:25,908
Some prisoners were taken out to steak dinners.

111
00:04:25,928 --> 00:04:28,120
Others were drawn into games.

112
00:04:29,425 --> 00:04:31,729
Henry Kolm, a young physicist who later

113
00:04:31,749 --> 00:04:33,721
worked at the Massachusetts Institute of

114
00:04:33,741 --> 00:04:36,377
Technology, said that a game of chess or

115
00:04:36,397 --> 00:04:38,701
Ping-Pong got more information out of a

116
00:04:38,721 --> 00:04:40,693
German general than torture does today.

117
00:04:40,713 --> 00:04:43,017
Arno Mayer, who later became a historian

118
00:04:43,037 --> 00:04:45,673
at Princeton, said his job was to keep

119
00:04:45,693 --> 00:04:47,665
certain prisoners happy, because the Army

120
00:04:47,685 --> 00:04:50,321
hoped they would come to work for the

121
00:04:50,341 --> 00:04:51,141
United States.

122
00:04:52,325 --> 00:04:54,085
For the most stubborn men,

123
00:04:54,105 --> 00:04:56,577
there was a harder card to play.

124
00:04:56,597 --> 00:04:58,713
Interrogators threatened to hand them over

125
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:00,137
to the Soviet Union.

126
00:05:00,157 --> 00:05:01,917
To make the threat believable,

127
00:05:01,937 --> 00:05:03,341
Russian-speaking American soldiers dressed

128
00:05:03,361 --> 00:05:06,901
up as officers of the Red Army and sat in

129
00:05:06,921 --> 00:05:07,969
on the questioning.

130
00:05:07,989 --> 00:05:11,173
It was a bluff, and for some prisoners it

131
00:05:11,193 --> 00:05:11,993
was enough.

132
00:05:13,575 --> 00:05:15,555
By the end of August 1945,

133
00:05:15,575 --> 00:05:17,412
the people of P.O.

134
00:05:17,432 --> 00:05:20,662
Box 1142 had written more than five

135
00:05:20,682 --> 00:05:21,591
thousand reports.

136
00:05:21,611 --> 00:05:23,912
Interrogations helped point Allied bombers

137
00:05:23,932 --> 00:05:27,162
at Peenemünde, the German rocket base on

138
00:05:27,182 --> 00:05:28,575
the Baltic coast.

139
00:05:29,975 --> 00:05:31,714
Interrogations and recordings revealed

140
00:05:31,734 --> 00:05:34,352
that the Germans had started loading

141
00:05:34,372 --> 00:05:36,110
supplies at railway crossings,

142
00:05:36,130 --> 00:05:38,749
because the stations were being bombed.

143
00:05:38,769 --> 00:05:41,826
They revealed that the German navy had

144
00:05:41,846 --> 00:05:44,464
built dummy concrete structures at its

145
00:05:44,484 --> 00:05:47,102
submarine pens, to fool Allied bombers.

146
00:05:47,122 --> 00:05:50,180
Both discoveries changed where the bombs fell.

147
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:52,379
Questioning U-boat crews about Germany's

148
00:05:52,399 --> 00:05:55,017
acoustic torpedo also helped the Americans

149
00:05:55,037 --> 00:05:57,235
develop a defense against it.

150
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,153
One prisoner never left.

151
00:06:01,173 --> 00:06:04,713
Werner Henke commanded the U-boat U-515.

152
00:06:04,733 --> 00:06:08,668
In December 1942, his boat sank the

153
00:06:08,688 --> 00:06:10,599
British liner Ceramic, and British radio

154
00:06:10,619 --> 00:06:13,175
later accused him of firing on the survivors.

155
00:06:13,195 --> 00:06:15,428
Henke believed the British would put him

156
00:06:15,448 --> 00:06:17,380
on trial as a war criminal.

157
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:21,893
He was captured in April 1944 and brought

158
00:06:21,913 --> 00:06:23,173
to Fort Hunt.

159
00:06:23,193 --> 00:06:24,879
According to his biographer,

160
00:06:24,899 --> 00:06:27,865
the interrogators used his fear of the

161
00:06:27,885 --> 00:06:29,145
British against him.

162
00:06:29,165 --> 00:06:33,368
On June fifteenth, 1944, Henke ran to the

163
00:06:33,388 --> 00:06:35,534
fence and started to climb.

164
00:06:35,554 --> 00:06:38,566
He kept climbing after the guards ordered

165
00:06:38,586 --> 00:06:42,465
him to stop, and a guard shot him dead.

166
00:06:42,485 --> 00:06:45,064
No prisoner ever escaped from P.O.

167
00:06:45,084 --> 00:06:45,950
Box 1142.

168
00:06:47,050 --> 00:06:49,175
Fort Hunt had a second secret,

169
00:06:49,195 --> 00:06:51,676
kept even from its own commanding officer.

170
00:06:51,696 --> 00:06:53,821
In another part of the post,

171
00:06:53,841 --> 00:06:56,323
a separate team built escape kits for

172
00:06:56,343 --> 00:06:58,130
American prisoners held in Germany.

173
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,922
They hid compasses inside uniform buttons,

174
00:07:01,942 --> 00:07:03,551
five million of them.

175
00:07:03,571 --> 00:07:07,214
They printed maps on silk and on tissue paper.

176
00:07:07,234 --> 00:07:10,064
They packed radio parts into baseballs and

177
00:07:10,084 --> 00:07:12,506
maps into decks of playing cards,

178
00:07:12,526 --> 00:07:16,169
and sent it all to German prison camps in

179
00:07:16,189 --> 00:07:18,205
care packages from relief organizations

180
00:07:18,225 --> 00:07:19,833
that did not exist.

181
00:07:19,853 --> 00:07:23,204
By 1944, eighty to a hundred and twenty

182
00:07:23,224 --> 00:07:25,080
packages were going out every day.

183
00:07:26,750 --> 00:07:30,650
When the war ended, so did P.O.

184
00:07:30,670 --> 00:07:31,770
Box 1142.

185
00:07:31,790 --> 00:07:35,327
In August 1945, the War Department ordered

186
00:07:35,347 --> 00:07:37,199
the escape program's records destroyed.

187
00:07:37,219 --> 00:07:40,567
The men who had worked there were sworn to

188
00:07:40,587 --> 00:07:44,746
secrecy, and in 1946 the barracks were bulldozed.

189
00:07:44,766 --> 00:07:47,990
By 1948, the land was a park again.

190
00:07:49,225 --> 00:07:51,515
Most of the veterans kept the oath.

191
00:07:51,535 --> 00:07:53,495
They did not tell their wives,

192
00:07:53,515 --> 00:07:54,485
or their children.

193
00:07:54,505 --> 00:07:57,455
As Arno Mayer put it, when you swear to

194
00:07:57,475 --> 00:07:59,765
secrecy, you don't really know when the

195
00:07:59,785 --> 00:08:00,585
oath expires.

196
00:08:02,225 --> 00:08:04,540
Sixty years later, the National Park

197
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:06,875
Service started asking questions about the

198
00:08:06,895 --> 00:08:07,654
old fort.

199
00:08:07,674 --> 00:08:10,767
It tracked down the men who had served

200
00:08:10,787 --> 00:08:12,713
there and recorded their memories.

201
00:08:12,733 --> 00:08:15,048
One ranger, Brandon Bies, interviewed more

202
00:08:15,068 --> 00:08:16,625
than seventy of them.

203
00:08:17,875 --> 00:08:20,626
In October 2007, about two dozen veterans

204
00:08:20,646 --> 00:08:21,418
of P.O.

205
00:08:21,438 --> 00:08:24,189
Box 1142 came back to Fort Hunt,

206
00:08:24,209 --> 00:08:27,357
most of them in their eighties and nineties.

207
00:08:27,377 --> 00:08:30,524
A flag was raised and a marker unveiled,

208
00:08:30,544 --> 00:08:33,315
at exactly eleven forty-two in the morning.

209
00:08:34,650 --> 00:08:36,808
They came back during another war,

210
00:08:36,828 --> 00:08:39,349
and another argument about how to question

211
00:08:39,369 --> 00:08:39,712
prisoners.

212
00:08:39,732 --> 00:08:42,979
Several of them had something to say about it.

213
00:08:42,999 --> 00:08:44,067
George Frenkel, eighty-seven,

214
00:08:44,087 --> 00:08:46,608
said they had extracted information in a

215
00:08:46,628 --> 00:08:49,875
battle of wits, and that he was proud he

216
00:08:49,895 --> 00:08:51,710
had never compromised his humanity.

217
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:55,160
If someone in your family served at Fort

218
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:57,500
Hunt, or ever spoke about a secret job in

219
00:08:57,520 --> 00:08:59,340
the war, tell us in the comments.

220
00:09:01,100 --> 00:09:03,426
Today, Fort Hunt is a park.

221
00:09:03,446 --> 00:09:06,163
Families have picnics in a pavilion built

222
00:09:06,183 --> 00:09:08,509
where the old post hospital stood,

223
00:09:08,529 --> 00:09:11,246
the building where the escape kits were

224
00:09:11,266 --> 00:09:12,028
once planned.

225
00:09:12,048 --> 00:09:13,983
The microphones are long gone.

226
00:09:14,003 --> 00:09:16,740
For sixty years, so was the story.

